# 🧵 Secure Coding Series #4: Authentication and Password Management – Lock It Right

## 🔐 What Is Authentication?

**Authentication** is the process of confirming that a user is who they claim to be.

But authentication done wrong = open doors for attackers.

Strong authentication ensures:
- Only the **right people** can access your system
- **Passwords** are securely stored and verified
- Attacks like **credential stuffing**, **brute-force**, or **session hijacking** are mitigated

---

## ❌ Common Mistakes in Authentication

| Mistake                          | Risk                                   |
|----------------------------------|----------------------------------------|
| Storing passwords in plain text  | Full compromise if database is leaked  |
| Weak password hashing            | Easy to crack (e.g., MD5, SHA1)         |
| Not limiting login attempts      | Vulnerable to brute-force attacks      |
| Hardcoded credentials in code    | Passwords leaked via code repos        |
| No 2FA                           | Easier account takeover                |

---

## ✅ Best Practices for Authentication

### 🔑 Password Storage

- **Never** store passwords as plain text
- Always use a **strong hashing algorithm** with salt:
  - Argon2 (best)
  - bcrypt (very good)
  - scrypt (also good)

#### Example – Python (bcrypt)
(python)
import bcrypt

# Hashing
password = b"secret123"
hashed = bcrypt.hashpw(password, bcrypt.gensalt())

# Verifying
bcrypt.checkpw(b"secret123", hashed)  # returns True


🛡️ Rate Limiting & Lockouts

Prevent brute-force attempts by:
	•	Locking account after X failed attempts
	•	Adding delay between failed logins
	•	Using CAPTCHA where appropriate


📲 Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA)

Always offer MFA (e.g., TOTP, SMS, or hardware key).
Even if the password is compromised, the attacker still needs the second factor.

🔐 Session Management
	•	Use secure, random session tokens (UUID or better)
	•	Store tokens in HttpOnly, Secure, and SameSite=Strict cookies
	•	Invalidate session on logout or password change


🏥 Real-Life Scenario: Hospital Staff Login System

📘 Context

You’re developing a web application for a hospital’s internal portal, where:
	•	Doctors and nurses log in to view patient records
	•	Admins log in to manage users and system settings

🧨 Problem

If passwords are stored in plain text and there’s no protection against brute-force login attempts, then:
	•	A data breach exposes passwords of all users
	•	Attackers can log in by trying hundreds of common passwords
	•	Sensitive medical records are at risk (HIPAA/GDPR violations)

✅ Secure Solution in C#

Let’s securely store and validate passwords using ASP.NET Core + BCrypt.

🔐 C# Code (Password Hashing & Login)

(C#)
using BCrypt.Net;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;

public class AuthController : Controller
{
    // Simulated DB record
    private static string storedHashedPassword = BCrypt.Net.BCrypt.HashPassword("SuperSafe123!");

    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult Login(string username, string password)
    {
        // TODO: Fetch real hash from DB using username
        var isPasswordValid = BCrypt.Net.BCrypt.Verify(password, storedHashedPassword);

        if (isPasswordValid)
        {
            // Success – generate session token, redirect to dashboard
            return Content("Login successful.");
        }
        else
        {
            // Optional: Track failed attempts, delay response
            return Unauthorized("Invalid username or password.");
        }
    }
}


🔒 Key Points
	•	BCrypt.HashPassword() is used during user registration
	•	BCrypt.Verify() checks input against the stored hash
	•	Password hashes are never reversed or decrypted
	•	No password is stored or logged in plain text
	•	Brute-force can be slowed down using response delays, lockouts, or rate-limiting

